The fast lane beside PRoot: a library preloaded into guest programs
(patches/proot/fastpath) answers stat, statx, access, readlink, read-only
open, fstat and close itself for paths that stay inside the rootfs. PRoot
hands it the rootfs, the bindings and the fake ids through prctl(PR_PROOT_FASTPATH), and its own idea of the
working directory, since chdir is emulated. The seccomp filter lets a
traced syscall made from the library's one fixed syscall instruction pass
untraced.

What the library remembers goes stale, so PRoot counts what matters in a
small file all guest processes map, "<rootfs>.fastpath" or
PROOT_FASTPATH_STATE, shared by every PRoot on that rootfs: symlinks made
and directories renamed, changes of fake ids, of the root and of the
bindings (chroot and mount are emulated), changes of a working directory.

As the guest's root, PRoot forces permissions on what a path walks
through and restores them when it next hears from the tracee. With the
fast lane that can be many library-made syscalls later, which would see
the forced mode; so such a syscall is stopped at its exit too, and the
mode restored there.

PROOT_FASTPATH=off turns all of it off, and so does the f2fs workaround.

